{"answer":"No — the current design in guard.py checks X-Small-Proxy before anything else, including the websocket Upgrade path. In Guard._handle(), the header check (`self.headers.get("X-Small-Proxy") != SECRET`) happens first and returns 403 immediately if it fails; only after that does it check for `Upgrade: websocket` and branch to `_websocket()` (guard.py lines 40-49). This means every request, including upgrade handshakes, must carry the shared secret before the proxy will even look at the Upgrade header.

This is also explicitly covered by test_guard_ws.py: it sends an upgrade request with no X-Small-Proxy header and asserts the response is 403, with the comment 'no header on the upgrade request: wall holds' (lines 83-88). That test is written specifically to lock in the behavior you're proposing to change.

So reordering to accept the Upgrade before validating X-Small-Proxy would contradict the recorded design and break that test's expectation — it would let unauthenticated clients reach the websocket tunnel path. If you need to change this, you'd want to update both guard.py's ordering and the test's expected behavior, and document why the auth requirement is being relaxed for upgrades (no such rationale is currently recorded).","citations":[{"kind":"code","path":"packages/runtime/guard.py","start_line":40,"end_line":49},{"kind":"code","path":"tests/unit_tests/packages/runtime/test_guard_ws.py","start_line":83,"end_line":88}]}
